.Dd August 27, 2026
.Dt C4-ARCHIVE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm c4-archive
.Nd C4 static library files
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The files used as static libraries with C4 use the native format of the system
.Xr ar 1
utility. This is some descendant of either a
.Bx
or SysV/GNU style archive tool
.Pq even on Windows .

While these files usually have the suffix
.Pq extension in other parlance
of
.Qq .c4a
to signify they are not ordinary archives. They, however, are in almost all
cases usable with tools designed for normal archive files. One needs to pay
attention as to not accidentally strip out the metadata described below, as some
operations with C4 tools require it to be present for convenient use, even if
in all cases it can be worked around.

.Ss Metadata sections
The C4 archive specifies some sections prefixed with
.Qq .c4
to store extraneous information in these object files and archives. When
consuming archives for any use, all object files are to be looked up in an
archive. This allows creating archives even with the native system archiver
.Pq that is Xr ar 1
instead of the C4 tools'
.Xr c4ar 1
utility. All fields are interpreted as if they were concatenated. The
.Xr c4ar 1
tool mentioned above physically strips and concatenates these for easier
processing into a fabricated object file, which results in equivalent behavior.

These sections always use the target's native object file format
.Pq eg. Xr elf 5 for most Ux descendants
as the carrier within archives.

The following sections are understood:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy .c4xport
A list of symbols
.Pq functions
exported from this object file. They are encoded in a binary format that is the
following:
.Bl -tag -width "length"
.It Va length
The length of the following symbol name in 32bit little endian unsigned integer.
.It Va name
The name of the symbol using the number of bytes described by length. It is
.Em NOT
.Dv NUL
terminated.
.It Va arity
The arity of the symbol in a 32bit little endian unsigned integer.
.It Va metadata
The metadata is two bytes, stored as a 16bit little endian unsigned integer. The
following information is encoded in the bits by index \(en after converting to
native format.
.Bl -tag -width x-xx
.It 0
Set if the symbol is a native symbol.
.It 1
Set if the symbol is a binary operator.
.It 2
Set if the symbol is a left associative binary operator.
.It 3\(en8
Reserved, contain garbage. Or the recipe for immortality. We'll never know.
.It 9\(en15
Set to the precedence of a binary operator. The maximum value is 100, but all
seven bits are reserved for this purpose because of obvious reasons.
.El
.El
.It Sy .c4mport
A long string of
.Dv NUL
terminated strings which refer to the names of the imported libraries when
compiling this project. If this does not exist or is empty, it is taken as no
.Ic use Ns -s
were made to libraries. The names are listed as used and not resolved.
.El

Note that fields are named in such ways to allow XCOFF and PE/COFF object format
compatibility used by AIX and Windows respectively. They don't spill out
section names shorter than 8 bytes into a separate string table, making
processing easier.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ar 1 ,
.Xr c4ar 1 ,
.Xr elf 5 ,
.Xr c4-use 7
.Sh HISTORY
The archive format's special fields got first introduced in C4 v0.3.0.
